Our experiments test whether FB's squared successor approximation can discard rare but control-critical action distinctions, and whether the \name{} preserves those discintions through normalization and the subsequent policy extraction process introduce in \cref{sec:method}.
In \cref{sec:exp-didactic}, we vary the the rarity of the bottleneck and the representation capacity for FB and \name{} for a set of didactic bottleneck MDPs with known action gaps and a closed form SM solutions to test whether FB loses the correct action-order, and which of \name{}'s design decisions help it preserve this ordering.
We then examine trained FB and \name{} policies in \cref{sec:exp-failure} to isolate the failure of FB in pick-and-place problems, testing whether errors in the learned critic's predictions coincide with these failures.
Finally, we evaluate whether these improvements demonstrated by \name{} translate to downstream manipulation performance across OGBench~\citep{park-iclr25a}, and offline datasets collected in MetaWorld~\citep{mclean2025metaworld}, and ManiSkill~\citep{taomaniskill3} (\cref{sec:exp-manipulation}).

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
All methods use states, are trained for $10^6$ gradient steps on the same fixed offline dataset.
We report results over $5$ independent training seeds.
For offline policy learning, we use the actor from \citet{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.
Success rates are averaged over the final three evaluations, with $10$ episodes per task.
Full implementation details are given in \cref{app:implementation}.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against two groups of methods.
\begin{inparaenum}
    \item we consider methods that learn reusable representations from the offline dataset: \textbf{FB}~\citep{touati-neurips21a,touati-iclr23a}, our primary baseline, together with \textbf{HILP}~\citep{park-iclr24a} and \textbf{RLDP}~\citep{jajoo-iclr26a}. These comparisons test whether the manipulation failure is specific to FB's successor approximation or is shared by alternative geometric and predictive representations. They are all trained using a task-independent representation through their backward embedding, and during inference, we specify the downstream goal reward on $10^4$ offline transitions and use these reward-labeled transitions only to infer the corresponding task vector and extract a policy. 
    \item We compare against goal-conditioned offline RL methods: \textbf{GCIQL}, \textbf{CRL}, and \textbf{QRL}. These methods train directly for goal reaching using hindsight goals sampled from the offline data, and help us exmaine how well the zero-shot policies compare with methods trained explicitly for goal-reaching control. 
\end{inparaenum}


\subsection{Identifying the bottleneck pathology}
\label{sec:exp-didactic}
%%AZ.9.12: this is the env where you should also conduct the ablations on Density-FB.
At a control bottleneck, the critic has to separate the values of the competing actions.
We test \cref{lem:linear-action-difference} on the process of \cref{fig:didactic-mdp} and on five bottleneck processes drawn at random (\cref{app:bottleneck-family} gives the generator and the graphs), with exact successor measures, a fixed continuation policy, discount $0.8$, and reward $1$ on arrival at the goal.
Every experiment reports the fraction of the exact action gap at the decision state that a critic reads, $\widehat G_r/G_r$.

