\paragraph{Which directions does the squared objective keep?}
FB chooses its features by minimizing the squared reconstruction error summed over all state-action rows, so the two rows of the decision state contribute in proportion to their weight, while the gap they carry does not depend on that weight at all.
\Cref{app:bottleneck-proof} shows that as this weight goes to zero the selected span converges to the one the ordinary rows would choose on their own, and the retained gap to the projection of $\Delta m$ onto that span.
\Cref{fig:bottleneck-main} (left) reduces the weight of the decision rows at rank $3$.
FB keeps the full gap while those rows carry a quarter or more of the objective, keeps $18\%$ of it at one sixteenth, and loses it entirely below that; at the limit its retained fraction is the projection the ordinary rows select, which on this process is zero.
At the same rank the normalized model keeps the full gap at every weight, including the limit in which the decision rows have no weight at all.
Linear squared approximation spends its limited capacity where the squared mass is, and a rare decision is not where the mass is.

\paragraph{How much capacity is needed to keep the decision?}
We therefore vary the representation rank on the five generated processes (\cref{fig:bottleneck-main}, right).
Giving FB more rank eventually fixes the problem: the exact successor measure contains the information needed to choose the right action, and the failure is in which part of it a low-rank squared fit keeps.
FB reaches $90\%$ of the gap at a median rank of $7$ across the five processes, \name{} at a median rank of $3$ (\cref{tab:family-rank}).

\paragraph{Which change in \name{} preserves the decision?}
We next ablate the three design decisions of \name{} on the bottleneck processes (\cref{fig:ablation}).
Merely normalizing FB's target only rescales the same squared regression problem, and its population optimum coincides with FB's at every rank.
Once the bilinear score is placed inside a softmax, the gap is preserved for both the squared loss and cross-entropy.
Thus the main gain comes from representing the SM as a normalized distribution.
We use the categorical objective because it gives the Bellman update derived in \cref{sec:method}; on these processes the two softmax losses are level from rank $3$ on, and the squared loss is ahead at rank $2$.

