\subsection{Isolating the Failure of FB}
\label{sec:exp-failure}

We next analyze whether the mechanism of \cref{lem:linear-action-difference} holds beyond the didactic MDPs by investigating where FB fails in the task of picking and placing the cube at a desired position. 
Following \citet{mohan-rlbrew26a}, we categorize each rollout episode by its first failed interaction phase: approach, acquisition, maintain, transport, or success.
In \cref{fig:failure-where}, the left panel shows that FB's failures occur predominantly after acquisition, during maintain and transport and \name{} mitigates the failures that occur once the cube securely held.
We then test whether this reflects critic error by restoring the simulator states from the approach, in-hand, and carrying stages and compare each critic's predicted future cube-position distribution with rollouts of its own policy. 
We additionally compare its ranking of candidate actions against their Monte-Carlo values.
The right panels of \Cref{fig:failure-why} show that FB's predicted futures remain close to the dataset distribution rather than those induced by its current policy, and its action rankings are also near chance, including at carrying states where action choice determines whether the object remains secured.

