If the bottleneck pathology identified in \cref{sec:failure} is relevant to manipulation, then \name{}'s representation should produce improvements in performance on tasks requiring precise object interactions.
We test this across 11 tasks from OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill (\cref{tab:manipulation}); dataset details are provided in \cref{app:data-mw-ms}.
Across all three benchmarks, \name{} consistently improves over FB, with average gains of $36.9$, $43.6$, and $29.7$ percentage points on OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill, respectively, and outperforms both HILP and RLDP on every task.
On OGBench, where goal-conditioned baselines are also available, \name{} exceeds GCIQL on \texttt{cube-single} and \texttt{scene}, while GCIQL performs better on \texttt{cube-double}.
\cref{tab:exorl} reports ExoRL locomotion, which has no object variable and lies outside the regime \cref{sec:failure} describes. \name{} is the best method on \texttt{point-mass}, where reaching a region of state space is the task, and is above FB on \texttt{quadruped}, while it matches RLDP and trails FB and HILP on \texttt{walker}: the density objective helps where reaching a state is the bottleneck and is not expected to help on dense velocity rewards.
This comparison is particularly stringent because GCIQL, CRL, and QRL are trained explicitly for goal reaching, whereas \name{} learns a task-independent successor representation and receives the downstream reward only through $10^4$ reward-labeled offline transitions used for post-hoc task inference.


